        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Decoding the Label on a Mold Product
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look first for the registration number. It appears as EPA Reg. No. followed by digits, usually near the ingredient statement. A product with a registration can legally claim to kill specific organisms, and the label lists which ones. A product without one can clean, remove stains, or deodorize, but it is not allowed to claim it kills mold. That does not make it useless; it just tells you what job it is built for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, find the contact time. It is the period the surface must stay wet for the kill claim to hold, and it is often buried in the directions. Ten minutes is common for mold claims. Spray-and-wipe-immediately defeats the purpose. On walls and ceilings, that often means spraying, waiting a few minutes, and spraying again to keep the surface wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at the surfaces list. Labels state where the product is approved: hard nonporous surfaces only, or also porous materials like wood and drywall. Using a nonporous-only product on wood does not mean it will not do anything, but the claims do not apply, and the product may bleach or damage the material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, read the precautionary statements and the safety data sheet, which most makers post online. They tell you whether you need gloves and eye protection, how much ventilation is required, and what the product must never be mixed with. Bleach products carry stronger warnings than quats or thymol, and the difference is real in a small, poorly ventilated room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No contact time, no kill</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A fungicide works by staying in contact with the organism long enough to damage it. Follow the label's wet time exactly, rewetting the surface if it dries early. A quick spray and wipe mostly moves mold around.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Matching the Product to the Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nonporous surfaces such as glass, glazed tile, plastic, metal, and sealed countertops are the easy case. Mold sits on top, nearly any registered product kills it, and wiping removes the residue. Bleach sprays work well here and also take care of staining.
          </p>

          <p>
            Semi-porous surfaces such as painted drywall, sealed wood trim, and concrete need a product approved for them and a little more patience. Mold can push hyphae, its root-like threads, into paint films and the top layer of concrete. A non-bleach fungicide that soaks in, followed by gentle scrubbing with a soft brush, is usually more effective than bleach, which can lighten the surface while leaving growth just below it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Porous materials such as unsealed drywall, ceiling tile, carpet and pad, insulation, and upholstery are where cleaning often stops being the answer. The EPA advises that porous materials with significant mold growth may need to be discarded, because growth inside the material cannot be fully removed. Small surface spots on drywall can be cleaned, but if the paper face is soft, stained through, or crumbling, cut it out at least a foot beyond the visible growth and replace it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Structural wood such as studs, joists, and subfloor is a middle ground. Surface mold on framing can usually be cleaned and treated, often with light sanding where staining matters. Wood that is soft, punky, or crumbly has rot as well as mold and needs repair. Once cleaned, keep the wood below about 16 percent moisture content, checking with a meter, and it will not support new growth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bleach-vinegar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Truth About Bleach, Vinegar, and Peroxide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Chlorine bleach kills mold on hard, nonporous surfaces and is excellent at removing stains. Its weakness is porous material. Household bleach is mostly water, and on wood or drywall the chlorine tends to react at the surface while water soaks in, so it can whiten the stain while doing less to growth below. The EPA does not generally recommend bleach for routine mold cleanup, noting that some residual spores remain and that bleach fumes are hazardous.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distilled white vinegar is roughly 5 percent acetic acid, and research shows it inhibits some common household molds while being less effective on others. It is cheap and low-hazard, which makes it a reasonable option for light mildew on nonporous surfaces. It is not a registered fungicide, so its results are less predictable. Never combine it with bleach; the mix releases chlorine gas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three percent hydrogen peroxide from the pharmacy also has antifungal activity and does not leave toxic residue, though it can lighten some materials. Spray it on, let it sit for 10 minutes or so, then scrub and wipe. Like vinegar, drugstore peroxide is not labeled as a mold killer, so treat it as a household fallback rather than a primary treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Baking soda, borax, tea tree oil, and grapefruit seed extract appear on many cleaning blogs. Borax and baking soda help with scrubbing and leave a mildly alkaline residue; essential oils have some antifungal activity in lab settings. None is a substitute for a registered product on a real mold problem, and essential oils can irritate sensitive people. When the stakes are higher, use a product with an EPA number.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER MIX</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Bleach plus ammonia or acid makes toxic gas</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Bleach combined with ammonia releases chloramine vapors, and bleach combined with vinegar or other acids releases chlorine gas. Stick to a single product per session, rinse the surface before switching, and keep air moving out of the room.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Protective Gear and Ventilation
          </h2>

          <p>
            Disturbing mold sends spores and fragments into the air. For small DIY cleanups, EPA guidance sets a minimum of an N-95 respirator, long gloves extending to mid-forearm, and unvented goggles. Long sleeves and clothes you can wash right away are sensible. A simple dust mask does not filter spores well enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventilate, but do it carefully. Open a window and put a box fan in it blowing out, so contaminated air leaves the house instead of spreading to other rooms. Close interior doors, and turn off the central heating or cooling so the return ducts do not pull spores into the system. A HEPA unit from our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">mold air purifier rankings</a>, running nearby, captures spores that escape the work area.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bleach sprays need extra airflow. In a small bathroom with no window, run the exhaust fan, take breaks, and stop if your eyes or throat burn. People with asthma or other lung conditions should avoid doing mold cleanup or bleach work themselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bag debris before carrying it through the house. Cut-out drywall, insulation, and rags go into heavy contractor bags in the work area, which you then seal and carry outside. Wash your clothes and shower when you finish, and clean the work area with a HEPA vacuum rather than a standard vacuum, which can blow fine particles back into the air.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Clean Mold, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Step one is to fix the water. Find and stop the leak, condensation, or seepage that let mold grow, and dry the area. Cleaning before the source is fixed is wasted effort. In a basement, a dehumidifier from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> set to 50 percent usually brings the space down within a couple of weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step two is to set up. Put on the respirator, gloves, and goggles, close off the room, set up the window fan, and lay plastic on the floor if debris will fall. Mist the moldy surface lightly with water or the product to keep spores from going airborne when you start working on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step three is to kill. Apply the registered fungicide until the surface is wet, and keep it wet for the label contact time. On vertical surfaces, reapply as it runs off. Do not dry-brush or sand mold before treating it, which sends spores everywhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step four is to clean. After the contact time, scrub with a soft brush or cloth and wipe away residue. Use a stain remover on surfaces that tolerate it if the marks bother you. Remove and bag materials that cannot be cleaned. Step five is to dry thoroughly within 24 to 48 hours, using fans and a dehumidifier, and then apply an inhibitor such as Concrobium. Only after the surface is dry and treated should you prime or paint, using a mold-resistant primer where appropriate.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Key Numbers for a DIY Cleanup</p>
